WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.366
The Mandelbrot set is a

00:00:01.366 --> 00:00:02.399
perfect example

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:03.800
of how a simple rule

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.900
can produce incredible complexity.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.020
At its core,

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.429
the set is generated by

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:10.166
iterating a quadratic equation,

00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:11.406
a simple formula

00:00:11.406 --> 00:00:15.066
whose highest exponent is two.

00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:17.166
To iterate a quadratic equation,

00:00:17.166 --> 00:00:19.566
choose a value for the variable.

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:20.985
Plug it into the function,

00:00:20.985 --> 00:00:22.456
then take the output

00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:24.055
and feed it back in again

00:00:24.055 --> 00:00:27.000
and again.

00:00:27.066 --> 00:00:28.876
The study of how recursive functions

00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:30.537
like these change over time

00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:32.907
is central to a field of math

00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:34.866
called Complex Dynamical Systems.

00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:36.904
This subject came about with the goal

00:00:36.904 --> 00:00:38.666
of understanding the real world.

00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:40.017
What are the mathematics

00:00:40.017 --> 00:00:42.068
that underlie the physical world

00:00:42.068 --> 00:00:42.966
that we see?

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.023
Most important early example

00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:46.700
would have been the solar system.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:47.866
So dynamical systems,

00:00:47.866 --> 00:00:49.527
you have some space

00:00:49.527 --> 00:00:50.886
where you have some rule

00:00:50.886 --> 00:00:51.733
that's assigning,

00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:53.108
as time moves on,

00:00:53.108 --> 00:00:54.255
how that space evolves and

00:00:54.255 --> 00:00:55.680
how things move and change

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.266
in that space.

